Advanced Computational, Optimization, and Learning Frameworks for the Furuta Rotary Inverted PendulumModern Physics Simulation Engines and Multibody Dynamics EnvironmentsThe physical dynamics of the Furuta rotary inverted pendulum are characterized by kinematic coupling, an underactuated configuration space defined on the non-Euclidean manifold $S^1 \times S^1$, and open-loop instability about its upright equilibrium. Originating from the 1991 benchmark formulation by Katsuhisa Furuta at the Tokyo Institute of Technology, the mechanism isolates the fundamental balance challenges found in rocketry thrust-vector control, bipedal humanoid walking, crane payload sway mitigation, and personal balancing transporters. Accurate numerical simulation, offline trajectory synthesis, and data-driven policy learning require simulation engines capable of resolving stiff differential-algebraic equations without numerical dissipation or artificial coordinate drift. While academic mechatronics curricula frequently standardize on analytical ordinary differential equation solvers or MATLAB Simscape Multibody blocks, contemporary robotics engineering utilizes specialized simulation platforms that balance parallel throughput, analytical mechanics, and physical contact modeling.The landscape of modern simulation engines splits into three distinct architectural paradigms. MuJoCo focuses on continuous multi-body contact dynamics and minimal coordinate representations, making it effective for zero-shot sim-to-real transfer and continuous control benchmarking. The Drake simulation platform emphasizes analytical mechanics, symbolic computation, and sum-of-squares optimization, providing certified stability regions and mathematically verified invariant sets. NVIDIA Isaac Lab leverages massively parallel GPU simulation pipelines via PhysX, running thousands of environment instances concurrently to train complex reinforcement learning policies within short timeframes.MuJoCo: Fast Continuous Dynamics and Sim-to-Real FidelityMuJoCo (Multi-Joint dynamics with Contact) has established itself as a primary simulation environment for continuous control benchmarks and physical reinforcement learning. Formulated using generalized minimal coordinates, the engine eliminates the numerical drift and artificial joint separation common in maximal-coordinate game engines. The Furuta pendulum is modeled via an XML-based MJCF description featuring a fixed world anchor, an actuated revolute joint defining the horizontal arm ($\theta_0$), and an unactuated revolute joint defining the vertical pendulum link ($\theta_1$).MuJoCo resolves non-conservative joint behaviors through explicit parameters governing viscous damping, dry Coulomb friction, and actuator rotor armature inertia. This inclusion of reflected rotor inertia is essential, as the high angular accelerations of the base servomotor introduce significant inertial resistance into the horizontal joint dynamics. Using semi-implicit Euler or fourth-order Runge-Kutta numerical integration at sub-millisecond timesteps, MuJoCo executes at rates exceeding 50,000 steps per second on single CPU threads. Native Python bindings allow researchers to wrap the underlying C dynamic engine within standardized Gymnasium environments, exposing direct armature voltage or torque interfaces that match physical motor drivers.Drake: Analytical Mechanics, Sums-of-Squares Optimization, and Stability CertificatesDeveloped by the MIT Robot Locomotion Group, Drake approaches underactuated mechanics through analytical dynamics and convex optimization rather than sampling heuristics. Drake instantiates the rotary pendulum as a MultibodyPlant associated with a SceneGraph geometry engine, parsing either Universal Robot Description Format (URDF) or programmatic system definitions.The primary advantage of Drake lies in its mathematical certification tools. Classical Linear Quadratic Regulator (LQR) designs operate under the assumption of localized linearization, which leaves the true operational boundaries of the balancing controller unquantified. Drake addresses this limitation by automating the computation of certified Regions of Attraction (ROA) via Sums-of-Squares (SOS) polynomial optimization. By formulating polynomial approximations of the closed-loop pendulum dynamics, Drake’s MathematicalProgram interface solves semidefinite programs (SDPs) to certify invariant sublevel sets $V(x) \le \rho$ around the upright vertical equilibrium. Trajectories originating within this certified boundary are mathematically guaranteed to converge to the inverted configuration.Additionally, Drake provides native trajectory transcription routines, such as DirectCollocation, which convert nonlinear differential equations into constrained optimization problems. These routines solve for minimum-effort swing-up trajectories that respect motor torque and horizontal arm excursion limits, providing verified reference paths for tracking controllers.NVIDIA Isaac Sim and Isaac Lab: Parallelized GPU VectorizationWhen training model-free reinforcement learning policies, sample complexity poses a significant computational bottleneck. NVIDIA Isaac Lab addresses this by shifting physics simulation directly to GPU Tensor and CUDA cores, bypassing the latency of CPU-to-GPU data transfers. Using vectorized PhysX pipelines, Isaac Lab simulates thousands of independent Furuta pendulum instances in parallel within a single graphics workstation.Isaac Lab features two distinct design models: a Manager-Based workflow that decouples rewards, observations, and termination conditions into modular classes, and a Direct workflow that executes an optimized, single-class structure following the Gymnasium API. For low-degree-of-freedom mechanisms like the Furuta pendulum, the Direct workflow achieves aggregate simulation throughput exceeding 200,000 steps per second. This throughput allows on-policy algorithms like Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO) to converge in minutes rather than hours, while simultaneously executing wide-range domain randomization sweeps across physical link parameters and joint friction coefficients.Simulation PlatformDynamic FormulationCore Strengths for Underactuated SystemsComputational Bottlenecks & LimitationsPrimary Furuta Pendulum Use CaseMuJoCo[cite: 9, 10, 12]Generalized coordinates (Lagrangian, convex optimization)High contact and friction accuracy; lightweight; deterministic CPU/GPU physicsLacks native formal SOS polynomial stability toolingSim-to-real RL policy training; system identification calibrationMIT Drake[cite: 13, 14, 21]Analytical kinematics and multibody mechanicsFormal Lyapunov certificates; SOS optimization; native trajectory collocationSteeper learning curve; slower parallel multi-instance scalingRegion of Attraction computation; nonlinear trajectory optimizationIsaac Lab[cite: 11, 16, 17]Reduced articulation coordinates (PhysX GPU pipeline)Massively parallel environment instances ($>4096$); rapid RL policy convergenceHeavy software stack; requires modern NVIDIA RTX GPU hardwareLarge-scale domain randomization; sample-intensive RL algorithmsSimscape Multibody[cite: 1, 6, 7, 8]Physical network modeling (DAE solvers)Direct integration with Simulink, Stateflow, and Embedded Coder deploymentConstrained single-instance execution speeds; proprietary licensingClassical control design (PID/LQR); HIL digital twin dashboardsTrajectory Optimization, Differential Dynamic Programming, and Embedded NMPCWhile heuristic energy-shaping methods (such as the Åström-Furuta strategy) reliably generate swing-up maneuvers, they cannot enforce state constraints—such as keeping the horizontal arm within a safe workspace—or minimize control energy. Numerical trajectory optimization approaches formulate both the swing-up and stabilization of the Furuta pendulum as an optimal control problem (OCP) solved over finite time horizons.Rigid-Body Dynamics and Differential Dynamic Programming: Pinocchio and CrocoddylThe integration of Pinocchio and Crocoddyl provides an efficient framework for trajectory optimization on multi-body systems. Pinocchio computes rigid-body spatial vector operations—including the Recursive Newton-Euler Algorithm (RNEA) for inverse dynamics and the Articulated Body Algorithm (ABA) for forward dynamics—along with their analytical derivatives $\frac{\partial \text{ABA}}{\partial q}$ and $\frac{\partial \text{ABA}}{\partial v}$ using analytical C++ spatial algebra.Crocoddyl utilizes these analytical derivatives to solve discrete-time optimal control problems via Differential Dynamic Programming (DDP) and box-constrained iterative Linear Quadratic Regulation (iLQR):$$\min_{\mathbf{x}, \mathbf{u}} \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \ell_k(\mathbf{x}_k, \mathbf{u}_k) + \ell_N(\mathbf{x}_N) \quad \text{s.t.} \quad \mathbf{x}_{k+1} = \mathbf{f}(\mathbf{x}_k, \mathbf{u}_k), \quad \mathbf{u}_{\min} \le \mathbf{u}_k \le \mathbf{u}_{\max}$$During the backward pass, Crocoddyl evaluates dynamic quadratizations of the Hamiltonian around nominal state-action trajectories. This computation yields an updated feedforward control sequence $\mathbf{u}_k^*$ alongside a sequence of linear feedback gain matrices $\mathbf{K}_k \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times 4}$:$$\mathbf{u}_k(\mathbf{x}_k) = \mathbf{u}_k^* - \mathbf{K}_k (\mathbf{x}_k - \mathbf{x}_k^*)$$This formulation generates a time-varying linear feedback policy (LTV-LQR) directly along the swing-up trajectory, providing stabilization against disturbances before the system enters the upright equilibrium basin. Armand du Parc Locmaria and Pierre Fabre implemented this architecture in the open-source Armandpl/furuta platform, demonstrating that Crocoddyl generates minimum-time, torque-bounded swing-up sequences without requiring manual switching heuristic tuning.Direct Multiple Shooting and Algorithmic Differentiation: CasADi and IPOPTCasADi provides a symbolic framework for nonlinear optimization and automatic differentiation via forward and reverse mode computation graphs. In direct multiple shooting formulations, the continuous time interval $[0, T]$ is partitioned into $N$ shooting intervals. The system state vector $\mathbf{x}_k = [\theta_0, \theta_1, \dot{\theta}_0, \dot{\theta}_1]^T$ and control input $u_k = V_m$ serve as decision variables within a sparse Non-Linear Program (NLP):$$\min_{\mathbf{x}_0, \dots, \mathbf{x}_N, u_0, \dots, u_{N-1}} \sum_{k=0}^{N-1} \left( \Vert{}\mathbf{x}_k - \mathbf{x}_{\text{goal}}\Vert{}_{\mathbf{Q}}^2 + \Vert{}u_k\Vert{}_{\mathbf{R}}^2 \right) + \Vert{}\mathbf{x}_N - \mathbf{x}_{\text{goal}}\Vert{}_{\mathbf{P}}^2$$$$\text{subject to} \quad \mathbf{x}_{k+1} - \mathbf{\Phi}(\mathbf{x}_k, u_k) = 0, \quad k=0, \dots, N-1$$$$\mathbf{x}_{\min} \le \mathbf{x}_k \le \mathbf{x}_{\max}, \quad u_{\min} \le u_k \le u_{\max}$$The dynamic matching condition $\mathbf{x}_{k+1} - \mathbf{\Phi}(\mathbf{x}_k, u_k) = 0$ is enforced as an equality constraint, where $\mathbf{\Phi}$ represents a numerical integrator (e.g., Runge-Kutta 4) evaluated across the shooting node. Solvers like IPOPT (Interior Point Optimizer) solve the resulting sparse Karush-Kuhn-Tucker (KKT) systems using exact Hessians, computing dynamically feasible swing-up trajectories that keep horizontal arm excursion within safe physical bounds ($\vert{}\theta_0\vert{} \le \theta_{0,\max}$).Millisecond Nonlinear Model Predictive Control: acadosFor real-time embedded execution, solving full NLPs using IPOPT introduces unacceptable latency ($50\text{ ms} - 500\text{ ms}$), exceeding the mechanical stabilization window of inverted pendulums. The acados framework addresses this by implementing Sequential Quadratic Programming (SQP) and Real-Time Iteration (RTI) schemes in optimized C, utilizing the High-Performance Interior Point Method (HPIPM) solver and BLASFEO linear algebra kernels.Homburger, Frey, Diehl, and Reuter demonstrated real-time millisecond NMPC running onboard a physical Furuta pendulum platform. By executing a single SQP iteration per sampling interval (RTI scheme) with a prediction horizon of $T = 2.0\text{ s}$ across $N = 50$ stages, acados achieves feedback computation times between $1.0\text{ ms}$ and $3.5\text{ ms}$. This speed allows a single NMPC formulation to execute the continuous swing-up maneuver, handle state and torque inequality bounds, and transition smoothly into upright stabilization without requiring an external finite state machine or supervisory switching guards.To accelerate convergence in fast mechanical systems, acados integrates Anderson Acceleration (AA) within its SQP solver. Applied to the projected regularized exact Hessian formulation for the Furuta pendulum, Anderson Acceleration reduces the SQP iterations required to reach tight tolerances by more than 50% (from over 60 iterations down to under 30), adding negligible computational overhead (approximately $1.6\ \mu\text{s}$ per iteration).Optimization ToolProblem FormulationSolver Back-EndTypical Computation LatencyHardware Feasibility & Practical NotesCrocoddyl / Pinocchio[cite: 9, 31]Unconstrained / Box-constrained iLQR & DDPCustom analytical DDP back-end$5\text{ ms} - 25\text{ ms}$ (offline trajectory generation)Computes stabilizing feedback gains $\mathbf{K}_k$ suitable for onboard tracking.CasADi + IPOPT[cite: 32]Direct multiple shooting NLPPrimal-dual interior point filter (IPOPT)$50\text{ ms} - 500\text{ ms}$ (too slow for real-time loops)Ideal for offline trajectory generation, benchmark reference paths, and parameter sweeps.acados (RTI SQP)[cite: 32, 33, 36]Real-Time Iteration NMPC (Gauss-Newton / Exact Hessian)HPIPM with BLASFEO linear algebra$0.8\text{ ms} - 4.0\text{ ms}$ (closed-loop execution)Capable of real-time millisecond swing-up and stabilization onboard physical hardware.acados + Anderson Accel.[cite: 36, 41, 42]Accelerated SQP fixed-point formulationHPIPM + AA(1) memory updateCuts required SQP iterations in half; adds $1.6\ \mu\text{s}$ overheadAccelerates convergence under parametric variations and loose tolerance conditions.Reinforcement Learning Libraries, Policy Architectures, and Reward FormulationsReinforcement learning formulates underactuated balance as a continuous Markov Decision Process (MDP) defined across states $\mathcal{S}$, actions $\mathcal{A}$, transition dynamics $\mathcal{P}$, and rewards $\mathcal{R}$. Unlike localized linear feedback controllers that rely on small-angle assumptions, model-free deep actor-critic architectures learn global policies that handle both swing-up and balance within a single neural network.Software framework selection determines the trade-off between deployment modularity and algorithm transparency. Stable-Baselines3 (SB3) provides modular, production-tested implementations of continuous actor-critic algorithms like Soft Actor-Critic (SAC) and Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO), supplemented with standardized wrappers for state normalization, frame history, and Generalized State-Dependent Exploration (gSDE). In contrast, CleanRL provides self-contained, single-file implementations that remove framework abstractions, exposing gradient updates directly and simplifying custom loss alterations for robotics applications.Continuous Action Algorithms: SAC vs. PPOWhile deep reinforcement learning algorithms can balance underactuated systems in simulation, their performance characteristics diverge on physical platforms:Soft Actor-Critic (SAC): As an off-policy algorithm founded on maximum entropy RL, SAC optimizes both expected cumulative return and policy entropy $\mathcal{H}(\pi(\cdot\vert{}s))$. The entropy regularization term prevents premature convergence to local limit cycles, encouraging thorough exploration during the swing-up phase. SAC exhibits higher sample efficiency than on-policy alternatives, requiring fewer environment steps to learn stable balancing behaviors. In empirical benchmarks on rotary pendulum setups, SAC generates responsive control signals that recover effectively from large angular deflections.Proximal Policy Optimization (PPO): As an on-policy policy gradient algorithm, PPO constrains policy updates using a clipped surrogate objective. While PPO is more sample-intensive—often requiring GPU-accelerated parallel simulation environments like Isaac Lab to train quickly—it produces smoother control signals that are less prone to high-frequency motor chattering. Kevin Wood demonstrated that PPO policies trained in MuJoCo can be transferred to custom 3D-printed rotary inverted pendulum hardware.Observation Space Manifolds and Trigonometric EmbeddingsPassing raw joint angles $\theta \in [-\pi, \pi]$ directly to neural policy networks introduces artificial discontinuities at the $\pm \pi$ wrap-around boundary, disrupting gradient descent during swing-up. To maintain topological continuity across the state space, rotary angles are projected into two-dimensional Euclidean embeddings:$$\mathbf{s}_t = \begin{bmatrix} \cos\theta_0 & \sin\theta_0 & \cos\theta_1 & \sin\theta_1 & \dot{\theta}_0 & \dot{\theta}_1 \end{bmatrix}^T \in \mathbb{R}^6$$where $\theta_0$ is the base arm angle and $\theta_1$ is the pendulum link deflection. By projecting the angles into continuous sine and cosine components, the state manifold $S^1 \times S^1$ maps onto a continuous representation without numerical boundaries. For multi-stage tasks or multi-link systems (such as rotary double inverted pendulums), the state vector can be augmented with a discrete target contextual variable $EP \in \{0, 1, 2, 3\}$ that conditions the network on target equilibrium configurations.Reward Engineering and Action RegularizationThe structure of the scalar reward function directly influences both policy convergence and the physical longevity of the hardware. Discontinuous step rewards produce sparse gradient feedback and encourage high-frequency bang-bang actuator commands. Polynomial rewards can exhibit steep gradients near the vertical apex, leading to destabilizing policy updates.Empirical studies indicate that continuous trigonometric cosine rewards provide smooth, well-conditioned gradients across the entire swing trajectory:$$R_t = c_1 \cos\theta_1 - c_2 \theta_0^2 - c_3 \dot{\theta}_1^2 - c_4 \dot{\theta}_0^2 - c_5 u_t^2 - c_6 (u_t - u_{t-1})^2$$The action continuity regularization penalty $-c_6 (u_t - u_{t-1})^2$ penalizes rapid changes in commanded voltage. Without this term, policies tend to command alternating maximum voltage commands, which can cause excessive actuator heating and gear wear. Adding the action continuity cost smooths the commanded trajectories, producing motor voltage profiles comparable to classical LQR controllers.Managing reinforcement learning lifecycles relies on MLOps infrastructure like Weights & Biases (W&B). Integrating W&B into pipelines such as Armandpl/furuta enables tracking decomposed reward metrics, tuning learning rates and entropy coefficients via automated Bayesian sweeps, and checkpointing network models alongside environment metadata to ensure reproducible policy deployment.Sim-to-Real Transfer Methodologies and System IdentificationDeploying policies trained in simulation directly onto physical hardware exposes discrepancies known as the reality gap. These differences stem from unmodeled friction, bearing stiction, motor saturation, voltage drops, and communication latency. Bridging this gap requires combining empirical system identification with simulation-side domain randomization and transport delay modeling.System identification protocols extract the physical parameters needed to ground the simulation model. Decoupled decay tests isolate link dynamics: clamping the base arm while logging the pendulum's unforced oscillations allows logarithmic decrement analysis to extract viscous damping ($b_1$) and dry friction. Motor electromechanical constants are characterized by measuring terminal resistance ($R_m$) under locked-rotor conditions and recording the no-load saturation slope to identify back-EMF and torque constants ($k_m, k_t$). For higher accuracy, multi-frequency sinusoidal voltage sweeps drive the combined mechanism, using Levenberg-Marquardt nonlinear least-squares optimization to fit model parameters against physical response data.To handle remaining model mismatch, domain randomization perturbs simulation parameters at the start of each training episode. Link masses, centers of gravity, damping coefficients, and motor constants are sampled from uniform distributions centered around their nominal values. Adding Gaussian noise ($\sigma_\theta \approx 0.005\text{ rad}$, $\sigma_{\dot{\theta}} \approx 0.05\text{ rad/s}$) to state observations accounts for encoder quantization and finite-difference differentiation noise.Transport delay introduces another significant operational challenge on physical hardware. Microcontroller execution and serial communication over UART or USB inject control loop latencies between $10\text{ ms}$ and $30\text{ ms}$. Policies trained in zero-latency simulations assume instantaneous torque delivery and can become unstable when subjected to real-world lag. Incorporating a First-In-First-Out (FIFO) queue into the simulation step function buffers commanded actions by $\tau_{\text{delay}}$, training the network to anticipate system momentum and execute stable balancing maneuvers on hardware.Real-Time Telemetry, Hardware-in-the-Loop Logging, and Telemetry VisualizersWhile MATLAB App Designer provides an established dashboard for academic prototypes, open-source robotics telemetry tools offer higher streaming rates and lower computational overhead during hardware-in-the-loop (HIL) testing.PlotJuggler provides high-throughput time-series visualization for low-latency debugging. Connecting directly to serial UART, UDP sockets, or ROS 2 topics, PlotJuggler processes incoming telemetry at update rates exceeding $1\text{ kHz}$. Built-in Fast Fourier Transform (FFT) tools assist in tuning encoder velocity filter cutoff frequencies by revealing mechanical vibration and quantization noise spikes directly. Integrated Lua scripting enables online calculation of energy metrics and cumulative error integrals (IAE, ISE, ITAE) during live operation.Foxglove Studio offers a modular visualization platform centered around the open-source MCAP binary container format. MCAP structures telemetry into indexed, compressed chunks (using lz4 or zstd), preventing file corruption during abrupt shutdowns and enabling random seeking across datasets. Foxglove Studio synchronizes 2D time-series strip charts with an interactive 3D panel rendering the pendulum's URDF model in real time from incoming joint angles. Recorded experimental runs can be scrubbed and replayed to directly compare hardware trajectories against simulation baselines.The Rerun SDK provides multimodal data logging across synchronized timelines. Written in Rust with bindings for Python, C++, and Rust, Rerun allows firmware or host drivers to log numeric state trajectories alongside spatial coordinate transforms. The client can map observations across multiple temporal axes—such as simulation time, wall-clock time, and control step sequence—preserving alignment despite communication latency and facilitating policy evaluation.Telemetry PlatformSupported Ingestion Protocols3D Spatial VisualizationAnalytical FeaturesSuitable Phase in Project LifecyclePlotJuggler[cite: 57, 60]Serial (UART), UDP, WebSockets, ROS 2, CSV2D kinematic canvas (limited 3D)Real-time FFT, Lua math functions, time-derivative filtersLow-level firmware bring-up; encoder noise filtering analysisFoxglove Studio[cite: 57, 61]MCAP, WebSockets, ROS 1/2, Foxglove BridgeInteractive 3D scene (URDF model parsing)Synchronized time scrubbers, custom dashboards, log indexingHardware-in-the-Loop tracking; mission replay; dataset archivingRerun SDK[cite: 57, 64]Native Python/C++/Rust SDK, WebSockets, gRPCGPU-accelerated 3D kinematic tree renderingMulti-timeline scrubbing; mixed multimodal (scalar + transform) loggingSim-to-real transfer debugging; policy verificationMATLAB App Designer[cite: 1, 6, 56]serialport, UDP blocks, Simulink Real-TimeSimulink 3D Animation (VRML / X3D)Online IAE, ISE, ITAE computation; Stateflow mode indicatorsAcademic HIL delivery; baseline controller evaluationEmbedded Execution, Micro-ROS, and TinyML Deployment FrameworksDeploying control algorithms onto embedded microcontrollers requires meeting real-time execution constraints. While baseline academic projects often implement continuous state-space controllers directly within MATLAB Simulink and compile via Embedded Coder, alternative embedded stacks offer greater autonomy, reduced memory footprints, and broader framework compatibility.Micro-ROS extends the Robot Operating System (ROS 2) directly onto resource-constrained 32-bit microcontrollers, including the STMicroelectronics STM32 (ARM Cortex-M4/M7), Espressif ESP32, and PJRC Teensy 4.1. Running atop embedded RTOSs (such as FreeRTOS or Zephyr) or bare-metal firmware, micro-ROS communicates with an off-board Linux host using the Micro XRCE-DDS (DDS for eXtremely Resource-Constrained Environments) middleware. A dedicated micro-ROS node on the microcontroller runs hardware timer interrupts at $1\text{ kHz}$ to decode quadrature encoder signals, calculate low-pass filtered velocities, and compute motor commands. The same node publishes state updates on topics like /furuta/state (sensor_msgs/JointState) and subscribes to reference commands on /furuta/cmd_vel, integrating the platform with ROS 2 nodes, trajectory generators, and Foxglove visualization pipelines. To maintain control loop determinism, the high-frequency control loop runs within a high-priority hardware timer interrupt or dedicated RTOS task, decoupling it from the lower-priority micro-ROS serialization and transport tasks.Deploying deep reinforcement learning models onto low-cost microcontrollers requires policy distillation and memory compression. Large training architectures (e.g., Multi-Layer Perceptrons with $2 \times 256$ units) can be distilled into compact feedforward networks while preserving balancing performance. Henrique Ferrolho demonstrated that a fully functional rotary inverted pendulum balancing controller can be compressed into a Multi-Layer Perceptron containing two hidden layers (16 and 8 neurons with ReLU activations), totaling 689 floating-point parameters:$$\mathbf{h}_1 = \operatorname{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_1 \mathbf{s}_t + \mathbf{b}_1) \quad (\mathbf{W}_1 \in \mathbb{R}^{16 \times 6})$$$$\mathbf{h}_2 = \operatorname{ReLU}(\mathbf{W}_2 \mathbf{h}_1 + \mathbf{b}_2) \quad (\mathbf{W}_2 \in \mathbb{R}^{8 \times 16})$$$$u_t = \tanh(\mathbf{W}_3 \mathbf{h}_2 + \mathbf{b}_3) \quad (\mathbf{W}_3 \in \mathbb{R}^{1 \times 8})$$Once trained in simulation via SAC and fine-tuned, the network weights and biases are exported as flat constant C arrays (const float W1[16][6] = {...};) and compiled directly into bare-metal C++ firmware. When deployed to an 8-bit Arduino Nano (ATmega328P running at 16 MHz without a hardware FPU), inference executes within approximately $3.2\text{ ms}$, maintaining a stable $200\text{ Hz}$ control loop. On 32-bit platforms featuring hardware Floating Point Units—such as the ARM Cortex-M4 (STM32F4) or Cortex-M7 (Teensy 4.1)—the same inference executes in under $15\ \mu\text{s}$, leaving ample processing margin for higher-level estimation tasks.For model-based optimal control (such as LQR, Linear Time-Varying Kalman Filters, or explicit MPC), firmware implementations rely on optimized linear algebra libraries. As a header-only library, Eigen supports fixed-size matrix allocations (Eigen::Matrix<float, 4, 1>), which avoids dynamic memory allocation on the heap and ensures deterministic execution times. This enables onboard execution of discrete Kalman filtering updates:$$\hat{\mathbf{x}}_{k+1} = \mathbf{A}_d \hat{\mathbf{x}}_k + \mathbf{B}_d u_k + \mathbf{L}_k (\mathbf{y}_k - \mathbf{C}_d \hat{\mathbf{x}}_k)$$$$\mathbf{P}_{k+1} = (\mathbf{A}_d - \mathbf{B}_d \mathbf{K}_k) \mathbf{P}_k (\mathbf{A}_d - \mathbf{B}_d \mathbf{K}_k)^T + \mathbf{Q}_k$$and full-state feedback $u = -\mathbf{K} \mathbf{x}$ at rates exceeding $10\text{ kHz}$. For ARM Cortex-M targets, ARM’s CMSIS-NN library provides neural network kernels optimized for SIMD instructions. Quantizing trained RL policies from 32-bit floats to 8-bit integers (int8) using symmetric quantization reduces policy memory footprint by 75% and improves inference throughput on microcontrollers lacking hardware FPUs.Embedded FrameworkTarget Hardware ClassPrimary StrengthsProcessing Constraints & Trade-OffsPractical Role in Furuta Stackmicro-ROS[cite: 69, 70]32-bit MCUs (STM32, ESP32, Teensy 4.1)Native integration with ROS 2 ecosystem and Foxglove StudioSerialization overhead; requires off-board micro-ROS agentDistributed HIL operation, state publishing, external loggingTinyML / Bare-Metal C++[cite: 12]8-bit to 32-bit MCUs (ATmega328P to ARM)Minimal footprint ($<1\text{ KB}$ RAM); deterministic microsecond inferenceRequires manual offline export of network weight arraysAutonomous, standalone neural policy balance executionEigen C++ Header Library[cite: 31]32-bit MCUs with C++11 (STM32, Teensy)Efficient matrix operations for LQR, Kalman filtering, and LTV controlFlash size grows if dynamic matrix operations are misconfiguredEmbedded optimal state estimation and gain-scheduled trackingMATLAB Embedded Coder[cite: 1, 6]Supported targets (TI C2000, STM32, Arduino)Automated C code generation directly from Simulink and StateflowProprietary toolchain; less flexible low-level driver configurationBaseline academic control delivery; rapid initial validationArchitectural Comparison and SynthesisTo evaluate the software ecosystems available for the Furuta rotary inverted pendulum, the surveyed tools can be synthesized into three primary development paradigms:Paradigm A: The Classical Academic Model-Based Stack (Simulink / Stateflow / App Designer / Embedded Coder). This workflow centers on the MATLAB environment. Plant models are built analytically and verified in Simscape Multibody. Local stabilizing controllers (PID, Pole Placement, LQR) are paired with Åström-Furuta energy swing-up laws via a finite state machine implemented in Stateflow. Hardware-in-the-Loop telemetry streams to an App Designer dashboard. This paradigm offers rapid setup and automated verification, but remains constrained by proprietary licensing and higher runtime memory requirements on embedded targets.Paradigm B: The Trajectory Optimization & Nonlinear MPC Stack (Pinocchio / Crocoddyl / acados / Foxglove). This approach formulates control as a numerical optimization problem. Pinocchio and Crocoddyl generate dynamic swing-up trajectories and stabilizing gain matrices offline, while acados enables real-time millisecond NMPC running directly onboard. Real-time telemetry is recorded to indexed MCAP logs and visualized in Foxglove Studio or PlotJuggler. This stack enforces state and torque bounds systematically, though it requires greater numerical optimization expertise and careful tuning of real-time iteration solvers.Paradigm C: The Deep Reinforcement Learning & Sim-to-Real Stack (MuJoCo / Stable-Baselines3 / Domain Randomization / TinyML). Here, a single neural policy learns both swing-up and balancing behaviors end-to-end. The agent trains inside MuJoCo or Isaac Lab using SAC or PPO, with domain randomization and latency modeling applied to bridge the reality gap. The converged policy is distilled into a compact neural network (under 1,000 parameters) and deployed onto low-cost microcontrollers via bare-metal C++. While this eliminates the need for manual switching logic, achieving stable real-world transfer requires careful reward shaping and accurate system identification.Engineering DimensionParadigm A: Classical Model-BasedParadigm B: Trajectory Optimization & NMPCParadigm C: Deep RL & Sim-to-RealCore Software ComponentsMATLAB, Simulink, Stateflow, SimscapePinocchio, Crocoddyl, acados, CasADiMuJoCo / Isaac Lab, SB3 / CleanRL, TinyMLTelemetry & VisualizationMATLAB App Designer, Simulink 3DFoxglove Studio, PlotJuggler, MCAPRerun SDK, Weights & Biases, PlotJugglerMathematical FormulationLinear state-space + Åström-Furuta energyDirect Multiple Shooting, iLQR/DDP, RTI SQPContinuous MDP, Actor-Critic (SAC/PPO)Embedded FootprintModerate ($10\text{ KB} - 50\text{ KB}$ Flash via Coder)High (embedded QP solver: $20\text{ KB} - 100\text{ KB}$)Extremely Low ($<5\text{ KB}$ Flash, $<1\text{ KB}$ RAM)Target MicrocontrollerSTM32, TI Delfino, Arduino (Coder targets)32-bit ARM Cortex-M7, Linux SBC (acados)8-bit to 32-bit MCU (ATmega328P to STM32)State Switching RequirementRequires supervisory FSM (Stateflow)Smooth transition (unified OCP formulation)None (unified end-to-end neural policy)Constraint HandlingSaturation blocks; heuristic soft limitsExplicit state and actuator torque boundsSoft penalties embedded within rewardDisturbance RobustnessGuaranteed nominal LQR margins; narrow basinHigh (receding horizon re-planning)High within randomized distribution envelopeTechnical Synthesis and Implementation RoadmapA phased engineering roadmap structures the implementation from initial plant modeling through hardware deployment and performance evaluation.The development workflow begins with physical parameter identification and multi-body model verification. Link geometries and mass distributions are obtained through metrology and precision balance measurements. Decoupled unforced oscillation experiments determine joint damping ($b_1$) via logarithmic decrement calculations, while locked-rotor and no-load tests establish electrical resistance ($R_m$), back-EMF ($k_m$), and torque constants ($k_t$). These identified parameters populate unified URDF and MJCF model definitions, providing a consistent dynamic representation across simulation environments. The resulting multi-body models are validated by verifying that unforced simulation trajectories match analytical ODE solutions.Next, the control architecture progresses to baseline controller synthesis and simulation verification. The nonlinear equations of motion are linearized about the upright apex to yield state-space matrices, confirming full controllability and observability. Linear Quadratic Regulators (LQR) are synthesized using Bryson's rule to balance state deviation against control voltage limits. An Åström-Furuta energy-shaping controller is implemented alongside the balancing law to manage swing-up from the hanging rest position. These algorithms are integrated within a MATLAB Stateflow chart using angular capture guards and hysteresis thresholds to prevent chattering at the basin boundary.Work then transitions to trajectory optimization and telemetry integration. The baseline energy swing-up strategy is benchmarked against optimal trajectories generated via Crocoddyl or real-time NMPC using acados, evaluating trade-offs in arm excursion and current consumption. Communication firmware is configured to stream state observations over high-speed serial UART or micro-ROS pipelines. Connecting PlotJuggler allows immediate evaluation of encoder noise and filter delay, while Foxglove Studio provides synchronized time-series tracking and 3D URDF playback.Finally, the project implements learned control via deep reinforcement learning and embedded sim-to-real transfer. A custom Gymnasium environment wrapping the MuJoCo simulation trains an actor-critic agent (SAC or PPO) on a continuous cosine reward function with action continuity penalties. To prepare the policy for real hardware, domain randomization is applied across physical parameters alongside transport delay buffering to model serial latency. Once converged, the policy is distilled into a compact neural network (fewer than 1,000 parameters) and exported as static C++ arrays, allowing bare-metal deployment on microcontrollers to benchmark learned control directly against the hybrid Stateflow/LQR baseline.
